---
import Prose from '../../layouts/Prose.astro';
import Chord from '../../components/Chord.astro';
import { getDocsPage } from '../../data/docs-topics';

const page = getDocsPage('dictation');
---
<Prose title={page.title} description={page.description} path={page.path}>
  <p>Dictation types what you say at the cursor. It is an app, off until you switch it on in Settings, Apps, Dictation. While it is on, <Chord of="CmdOrCtrl+Shift+." /> or Dictate in the Edit menu starts it, and the status bar shows the microphone's level and how long it has been on. The same shortcut, or a click on the microphone in the status bar, stops listening; what you said before that is still inserted.</p>

  <h2 id="engines">Engines</h2>
  <p>On macOS 26 or later, the On this Mac engine transcribes on the Mac and sends no audio anywhere. Its speech model downloads the first time, and the status bar shows how far along it is. On other systems, or with Engine set to API, Jotra sends what you said at each pause to a transcription service: OpenAI with <code>gpt-transcribe</code>, Groq with <code>whisper-large-v3-turbo</code>, or any OpenAI-compatible server by its base URL, which most servers expect to end in <code>/v1</code>. Choosing a provider fills in its model, and a model you typed is kept. Groq bills each request as at least 10 seconds of audio.</p>

  <h2 id="key">Key and consent</h2>
  <p>The API key is kept in the system keychain on macOS and Windows and in the Secret Service on Linux. Where none is available, Jotra holds the key in memory only, and you enter it again the next time you open Jotra. While Chat or Rewrite is on and Dictation uses the same provider and address (scheme, host and port) as the AI connection, Dictation uses that connection's key and Settings shows no second key field. Otherwise Dictation keeps its own key, entered on its Settings page. A custom server's key is kept for its address, so a server at another address needs its own key. Before the first audio goes to a host, Settings names the host and asks once. Allowing a host for Chat or Rewrite does not allow it for dictation.</p>

  <h2 id="language">Language</h2>
  <p>Language is Automatic until you choose one. On the API engine, Automatic lets the service detect the language of each phrase, so one session can move between languages. On this Mac, Automatic means the Mac's system language, and the list holds only the languages the Mac can transcribe. Naming the language you speak makes transcription more accurate.</p>

  <h2 id="session">Stopping and undo</h2>
  <p>Each phrase is inserted when you pause, where the cursor was when you started, and each is its own undo step. Escape stops at once: what is not inserted yet is discarded and what is inserted stays. While Find, the palette, a dialog, a menu or the completion list is open, or the cursor is in a text field, Escape goes to that instead. Switching to another tab or switching Dictation off stops it the same way. Hiding Jotra on macOS (<Chord of="CmdOrCtrl+H" />), or hiding, minimizing or closing its window, stops listening the way the shortcut does, and so does a minute without speech. On Linux under Wayland a minimized window is not detected, so it keeps listening until the shortcut, Escape or a minute without speech ends it. With Settings, Apps, Clean up with the AI connection on, each phrase goes through the AI connection to fix punctuation and misheard words before it is inserted.</p>

  <h2 id="microphone">Microphone access</h2>
  <p>macOS asks the first time Jotra uses the microphone. If access was denied, turn Jotra on in System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security, Microphone; the message Jotra shows opens it. On Windows, desktop apps need microphone access on in Windows Settings, Privacy &amp; security, Microphone.</p>
  <p>On Linux, Jotra records from ALSA's default device through <code>libasound2</code>, which the deb and AUR packages depend on. The AppImage takes it from the system and does not start without it, even with Dictation off: install <code>libasound2</code>, <code>libasound2t64</code> on Ubuntu 24.04, or <code>alsa-lib</code> on other distributions. On PipeWire or PulseAudio, that device comes from the <code>pipewire-alsa</code> or <code>pulseaudio-alsa</code> package; without it, no microphone opens.</p>
</Prose>
